# narasi versi 55 detik (user 21 Nov: narasi dipanjangin, info lebih lengkap). C = penutup.
C=" Comment below, and subscribe for more!"
N55={
'thanksgiving_dyk':"What if Thanksgiving happened on a totally different day? Let's check around the world. America: the fourth Thursday of November, with turkey, pumpkin pie and football. Canada: the second Monday of October, more than a month earlier! Germany: a harvest festival called Erntedankfest, in early October, with decorated churches. South Korea: Chuseok, a three day harvest holiday when families travel home. Japan: Labor Thanksgiving Day, every November twenty third, to thank workers. But here's the twist. Australia: on tiny Norfolk Island, people celebrate an American style Thanksgiving, a tradition started by American whalers! When do you give thanks?"+C,
'blackfriday_dyk':"Is Black Friday the biggest shopping day on Earth? Let's see. America: Black Friday, the day after Thanksgiving, when some stores open super early. Australia: Black Friday too, now one of its biggest sales of the year. The UK: Black Friday as well, borrowed from the US just over ten years ago. Brazil: Black Friday often stretches into a whole Black November! Mexico: El Buen Fin, its own big sales weekend in November, started in twenty eleven. But here's the twist. China: Singles' Day, on November eleventh, has sold more than Black Friday and Cyber Monday combined, mostly online! Do you shop on Black Friday?"+C,
'newyear_dyk':"What if New Year's Eve meant smashing plates? Let's look around the world. America: the famous ball drop in Times Square, watched by a million people in the cold. Australia: huge fireworks over Sydney Harbour, one of the first big cities to welcome the new year. Spain: eat twelve grapes, one for every bell at midnight! Japan: temple bells ring one hundred and eight times, one for each human desire. Brazil: wear white and jump over seven waves for luck. But here's the twist. Denmark: people smash old plates on their friends' doors, and the biggest pile means the most friends! How do you celebrate?"+C,
'emergency_dyk':"What if nine one one didn't work where you live? Here's who to call. America: nine one one, for police, fire and ambulance. Australia: triple zero, and you can also text the National Relay Service. The UK: nine nine nine, the oldest emergency number in the world, since nineteen thirty seven! New Zealand: one one one. Japan: one one nine for fire and ambulance, and one one zero for police. But here's the twist. Germany: one one two works all across the European Union, and many mobile phones will connect it in other countries too, often even without a SIM card! What's your emergency number?"+C,
'penny_dyk':"What if pennies just disappeared? It's already happening! New Zealand: one and two cent coins, gone in nineteen ninety. Australia: gone in nineteen ninety two, and prices are rounded to five cents. Finland: no one or two cent euro coins in everyday use, so prices are rounded instead. The Netherlands: rounding too, since two thousand four. Canada: said goodbye to the penny in twenty thirteen, because each one cost more than a cent to make. But here's the twist. America: the US Mint made its very last penny in twenty twenty five, after more than two hundred thirty years! Do you still use pennies?"+C,
'xmasholiday_dyk':"What if Christmas was just a normal work day? Let's check. America: Christmas Day is a federal holiday, and most stores close. Australia: two days off, Christmas and Boxing Day, right in the middle of summer! The UK: two days off too, Christmas and Boxing Day, famous for big sales. South Korea: Christmas is a public holiday, and it's a popular day for couples. China: Christmas is a normal work day, but malls still put up giant trees. But here's the twist. Japan: Christmas isn't a day off either, yet cities are covered in lights and families line up for fried chicken! Do you get Christmas off?"+C,
'voltage_dyk':"What if your charger didn't fit the wall? Let's travel with a plug. America: one hundred twenty volts, with a flat two pin plug. Canada: one hundred twenty volts too, with the same plug, so Americans can relax. The UK: two hundred thirty volts, and a chunky three pin plug with a fuse inside! Australia: two hundred thirty volts, with slanted pins that look like a surprised face. Switzerland: two hundred thirty volts, and its own unique plug. But here's the twist. Japan: just one hundred volts, and the country runs on two different electricity frequencies, fifty in the east and sixty in the west! What plug do you use?"+C,
'vat_dyk':"What if every price had a twenty five percent tax? Let's compare. America: no national sales tax, just state and local taxes, and some states have none at all. Canada: a five percent federal GST, plus provincial taxes. Japan: a ten percent consumption tax, with eight percent on most food. Australia: a ten percent GST, already included in the price, so no surprises at the register. The UK: twenty percent VAT, included in the price too. But here's the twist. Denmark: twenty five percent VAT on almost everything, even food, and there are no lower rates at all! How much tax do you pay?"+C,
'dst_dyk':"What if the clocks never changed? Let's check. America: clocks change twice a year, except in Hawaii and most of Arizona. Australia: only some states change, so in summer, Queensland and New South Wales are an hour apart! The UK: yes, it switches to British Summer Time every March. Japan: no, the clocks never change, it only tried for a few years after World War Two. China: no, it stopped in nineteen ninety one. But here's the twist. Russia: tried permanent summer time in twenty eleven, people hated the dark winter mornings, and it gave up in twenty fourteen! Should clocks still change?"+C,
'timezones_dyk':"Which country has the most time zones? Let's count. China: just one time zone for the whole country, so in the far west the sun can rise after ten AM! India: one time zone too, and it's half an hour off from most of the world. Australia: three main time zones, plus a few unusual ones. America: six time zones across its fifty states, from Hawaii to the East Coast. Russia: eleven time zones, from Europe all the way to the Pacific! Think that's the most? Here's the twist. France: twelve time zones, thanks to its islands and territories all over the world! How many does your country have?"+C,
'dateformat_dyk':"Is December eleventh written twelve eleven, or eleven twelve? It depends where you live! America: month first, then the day, so it's twelve eleven. Australia: day first, then the month, so it's eleven twelve. The UK: day first too, which confuses a lot of American travelers. Japan: year first, then month, then day, which makes dates easy to sort. China: year first as well, the same as the international ISO standard. But here's the twist. Canada: you can find all three formats, sometimes on the same street, so a date like three four can mean two different days! How do you write the date?"+C,
'metric_dyk':"What if everyone used kilometers? Let's compare. America: miles, feet, pounds, and Fahrenheit, one of only three countries not fully metric. Canada: kilometers on the road, but people still say their height in feet! Australia: fully metric since the nineteen seventies. Japan: metric, but apartment rooms are often measured in tatami mats. India: switched to metric in the late nineteen fifties. But here's the twist. The UK: metric in shops, but road signs are still in miles, people weigh themselves in stones, and beer comes in pints! Miles or kilometers?"+C,
'islands_dyk':"Which country has the most islands? Let's count up. The Philippines: about seven thousand six hundred islands, and some only appear at low tide. Australia: over eight thousand two hundred, including the famous Fraser Island, now K'gari. Japan: about fourteen thousand, after a new count in twenty twenty three. Indonesia: more than seventeen thousand! America: around eighteen thousand six hundred, counting Alaska and Hawaii. Think that's a lot? Here's the twist. Sweden: about two hundred sixty seven thousand islands, the most in the world, and most have nobody living on them! How many islands does your country have?"+C,
'hottest_dyk':"Where is the hottest place on Earth? Let's turn up the heat. The UK: forty point three degrees Celsius, in July twenty twenty two. Spain: forty seven point six, in twenty twenty one. Canada: forty nine point six, in the town of Lytton, which burned down the next day! Australia: fifty point seven, in Oodnadatta. Kuwait: fifty three point nine, one of the hottest readings in Asia! Think that's hot? Here's the twist. America: Death Valley hit fifty six point seven degrees in nineteen thirteen, that's one hundred thirty four Fahrenheit, the hottest ever recorded on Earth! What's the hottest it gets where you live?"+C,
'coldest_dyk':"How cold can it get where people live? Let's find out. Australia: minus twenty three degrees Celsius, in the Snowy Mountains. The UK: minus twenty seven point two, in Scotland. Norway: minus fifty one point four! America: minus sixty two point two, in Alaska. Canada: minus sixty three, in the Yukon, so cold that people's breath made a hissing sound! Think that's cold? Here's the twist. Russia: minus sixty seven point eight degrees in Oymyakon, and about five hundred people still live there all year, where kids only stay home from school when it drops below about minus fifty! How cold does it get where you live?"+C,
'biggest_dyk':"Is America the biggest country in the world? Let's measure. Australia: about seven point seven million square kilometers, the size of a whole continent. Brazil: eight and a half million, almost half of South America. China: nine point six million. America: nine point eight million, depending on how you count water. Canada: almost ten million, with more lakes than any other country! Think that's big? Here's the twist. Russia: seventeen point one million square kilometers, almost twice the size of Canada, and it spans eleven time zones! How big is your country?"+C,
'population_dyk':"Which country has the most people? Let's count. Australia: about twenty seven million, mostly living near the coast. Japan: around one hundred twenty four million, and the number is shrinking every year. Indonesia: about two hundred eighty million, spread over thousands of islands. America: around three hundred forty million, the third biggest population on Earth. China: about one point four one billion! Think China is number one? Here's the twist. India: about one point four five billion people, the biggest population on Earth since twenty twenty three, and half of them are under thirty! How many people live in your country?"+C,
'sheep_dyk':"What if there were more animals than people where you live? America: about eighty seven million cows, but way more people. India: over one hundred ninety million cattle, but about one point four billion people. The UK: around thirty one million sheep, about one for every two people. Ireland: about seven million cattle, more cows than people! New Zealand: more than four sheep for every person, although that number used to be over twenty! Think that's wild? Here's the twist. Australia: there are more kangaroos than people, with tens of millions hopping around a country of twenty seven million! What animal wins where you live?"+C,
'nationalanimal_dyk':"What if your national animal was a unicorn? Let's check. America: the bald eagle, and since twenty sixteen, the bison is the national mammal too. Australia: the kangaroo, right on the coat of arms next to the emu. Canada: the beaver, which is even on the five cent coin! New Zealand: the kiwi, a bird that can't fly, and New Zealanders are called Kiwis too. India: the Bengal tiger, and India has most of the world's wild tigers. But here's the twist. The UK: Scotland's national animal is the unicorn, and it's shown wearing a chain to look powerful! What's your national animal?"+C,
'tallestbuilding_dyk':"Where is the tallest building on Earth? Let's go up. The UK: the Shard in London, three hundred ten meters. Australia: Q one on the Gold Coast, three hundred twenty two meters. America: One World Trade Center, five hundred forty one meters, that's one thousand seven hundred seventy six feet, for the year of independence! China: Shanghai Tower, six hundred thirty two meters. Malaysia: Merdeka one eighteen, six hundred seventy nine meters! Think that's tall? Here's the twist. Dubai: the Burj Khalifa, eight hundred twenty eight meters, and you can see its tip from almost a hundred kilometers away! What's the tallest building near you?"+C,
'tuition_dyk':"What if college was free? Let's compare. America: about eleven thousand six hundred dollars a year at a public college, before aid, and much more at private schools. The UK: about nine thousand five hundred pounds a year in England. Australia: students can use a government loan, and pay it back only once they earn enough. Japan: about five hundred thirty six thousand yen a year at national universities. France: only about one hundred seventy five euros a year at public universities! But here's the twist. Germany: public universities are free, even for most international students, you just pay a small semester fee! How much is college where you live?"+C,
'schoolage_dyk':"What if school didn't start until you were seven? Let's compare. The UK: kids can start school at four in England, one of the youngest in Europe! Australia: around five, in a year called prep or kindy. America: kindergarten at five or six, depending on the state. Japan: elementary school at six, and kids often walk to school on their own. Germany: school at six too, with a giant cone full of sweets on the first day! But here's the twist. Finland: kids start school at seven, have short school days and little homework, and Finland is still famous for great education! When did you start school?"+C,
'summerbreak_dyk':"What if summer break lasted three months? Let's compare. Australia: about six weeks, over Christmas, because it's summer there! The UK: about six weeks, from late July to early September. Japan: about six weeks, from late July, but kids still get a lot of homework. Germany: six weeks, and each state starts at a different time so the highways don't jam. America: around ten to eleven weeks, depending on the state! Think that's long? Here's the twist. Italy: about thirteen weeks, from mid June to mid September, one of the longest summer breaks in Europe! How long is your summer break?"+C,
'cash_dyk':"What if cash disappeared? Let's see who still uses it. Germany: still loves cash, and many small shops and bakeries don't take cards! Japan: still uses lots of cash too, though phone payments are growing fast. America: most people pay by card now, but some cities require stores to accept cash. Australia: cash is only about thirteen percent of in person payments. The UK: around twelve percent. But here's the twist. Sweden: fewer than one in ten store purchases use cash, some shops won't take it at all, and almost everyone pays with an app called Swish! Cash or card?"+C,
'speedfine_dyk':"What if your speeding ticket depended on how much you earn? America: usually a flat fine, the same for everyone, set by each state. Australia: a flat fine too, plus demerit points on your license. The UK: courts can set fines based on your weekly income. Germany: day fines, based on income, for serious offenses. Switzerland: income based, and one driver got a fine of over one hundred thousand dollars! But here's the twist. Finland: one millionaire got a speeding ticket of one hundred twenty one thousand euros, for going thirty kilometers per hour over the limit! Should fines depend on income?"+C,
'gamble_dyk':"What if you weren't allowed into a casino in your own country? America: you usually need to be twenty one, and Las Vegas has more than forty big casinos. Australia: eighteen, and Australians lose more per person on gambling than anyone else. The UK: eighteen too. Japan: no casinos yet, the first one is planned in Osaka. Singapore: locals pay a one hundred fifty dollar entry fee every day, while tourists get in free! But here's the twist. South Korea: locals are banned from every casino except one, called Kangwon Land, up in the mountains! Would you go to a casino?"+C,
'trains_dyk':"What if America had bullet trains everywhere? Let's compare. Australia: no high speed rail at all, so most people fly between big cities. America: the new Acela reaches one hundred sixty miles per hour, but only on short stretches. Germany: ICE trains at three hundred kilometers per hour. France: TGV trains at three hundred twenty, and one test train hit five hundred seventy four! Japan: the Shinkansen at three hundred twenty too, famous for being on time to the second. But here's the twist. China: over forty five thousand kilometers of high speed rail, more than the rest of the world combined! Would you ride a bullet train?"+C,
'fries_dyk':"Are French fries actually French? Let's ask around. America: French fries, the side dish with almost every burger. The UK: chips, and potato chips are called crisps, so fish and chips means fries! Australia: hot chips, to tell them apart from the ones in a bag. Canada: poutine, fries with gravy and squeaky cheese curds, from Quebec. The Netherlands: patat, often with a big scoop of mayo! But here's the twist. Belgium: Belgians say fries were invented there, not in France, and they even have a fries museum in Bruges! What do you call them?"+C,
'toothfairy_dyk':"What if the tooth fairy was actually a mouse? Let's check. America: the tooth fairy leaves money under your pillow, about six dollars a tooth on average! Australia: the tooth fairy too. The UK: the tooth fairy as well, usually leaving a shiny coin. Mexico: Ratón Pérez, a little mouse, takes your tooth! France: la petite souris, the little mouse, hides a coin under the pillow. But here's the twist. Japan: kids throw their lower teeth up onto the roof, and upper teeth down under the house, so new teeth grow in straight! Who visited you, the fairy or the mouse?"+C,
'birthday_dyk':"What if your birthday came with a face full of cake? Let's see. America: cake, candles and the birthday song, which was under copyright until twenty sixteen! Australia: fairy bread, white bread with butter and sprinkles! Mexico: la mordida, someone pushes your face into the cake! Canada: in Atlantic Canada, kids get their nose greased with butter for luck! Denmark: flags hang in the window to tell everyone it's your birthday. But here's the twist. South Korea: until twenty twenty three, babies turned one on the day they were born, and everyone got a year older on New Year's Day! How do you celebrate?"+C,
'sunday_dyk':"What if every store closed on Sunday? Let's compare. America: most stores open seven days a week, some all night. Australia: open on Sundays too, though some stores open later. The UK: big stores can only open for six hours, usually ten to four. Switzerland: most shops stay closed, except at train stations and airports. Germany: nearly all shops are closed by law, so everyone rushes to the supermarket on Saturday! But here's the twist. Poland: shopping is banned on most Sundays, only a few Sundays a year are allowed, mostly before Christmas and Easter! Would you survive a Sunday with no shopping?"+C,
'greetings_dyk':"What if you said hello with your nose? Let's travel. America: a firm handshake, and a weak one can look rude! Australia: a handshake and a friendly g'day, mate! Japan: a bow, and the deeper the bow, the more respect you show. France: la bise, a kiss on each cheek, and some regions do three or even four! India: namaste, palms pressed together with a small bow, no touching needed. But here's the twist. New Zealand: the Māori hongi, pressing noses and foreheads together to share the breath of life! How do you say hello?"+C,
'mostvisited_dyk':"What if your country got a hundred million tourists a year? Let's count. Australia: about seven million visitors, mostly from New Zealand and China. Turkey: about fifty five million, many heading to Istanbul. Italy: about fifty seven million, for pizza, pasta and Rome. America: about sixty six million, with New York and Florida on top. Spain: about eighty five million, more tourists than people living there! But here's the twist. France: about one hundred million tourists, the most visited country on Earth, and the Louvre is the most visited museum! Which country would you visit first?"+C,
'football_dyk':"Is it soccer or football? The world can't agree. America: soccer, because football means the NFL, with helmets and touchdowns! Australia: soccer too, since footy usually means Aussie rules or rugby league. The UK: football, and the word soccer actually started there, as slang for association football! Brazil: futebol, and they've won five World Cups. Spain: fútbol, home of Real Madrid and Barcelona. But here's the twist. Italy: they call it calcio, which means kick, named after an old Florence game with almost no rules! What do you call it?"+C,
'breakfast_dyk':"What if breakfast was chocolate sprinkles on bread? Let's see. America: pancakes, eggs and bacon, with maple syrup on top. Australia: Vegemite on toast, salty and black! The UK: a full English, with beans, sausages, bacon, eggs and toast. Japan: rice, miso soup and grilled fish, a savory start to the day. Mexico: chilaquiles, tortilla chips cooked in salsa and topped with cheese. But here's the twist. The Netherlands: hagelslag, chocolate sprinkles on buttered bread, and the Dutch eat about seven hundred fifty million slices a year, grown ups too! What did you have for breakfast?"+C,
'dinnertime_dyk':"What if dinner started at ten at night? Let's check the clock. America: around six PM, sometimes even earlier with kids. Australia: around six thirty, and they often call it tea! The UK: around seven, and in the north it's also called tea. Germany: Abendbrot, a cold bread dinner around six or seven, since lunch is the big meal. Italy: around eight thirty, after an aperitivo. But here's the twist. Spain: dinner often starts at ten PM, or even later, partly because Spain uses a time zone that's an hour ahead of the sun! When do you eat dinner?"+C,
'bottle_dyk':"What if every empty bottle was worth money? Let's compare. America: only ten states pay a bottle deposit, like Michigan, with ten cents per can. Australia: ten cents per bottle in most states, and kids collect them for pocket money. Canada: deposits in most provinces, usually on drinks and beer. The Netherlands: deposits on bottles and cans, since twenty twenty three for cans. Germany: twenty five cents back for each plastic bottle, at machines in every supermarket! But here's the twist. Norway: over ninety percent of bottles come back, and some are recycled into new bottles again and again! Do you return your bottles?"+C,
'parentalleave_dyk':"What if new parents got more than a year off? Let's compare. America: no national paid leave, just twelve weeks unpaid, and only for some workers. Australia: twenty six weeks of government paid leave, shared between parents. The UK: up to thirty nine weeks of statutory pay. Norway: forty nine weeks at full pay, with fifteen weeks just for dad. Canada: up to eighteen months of benefits. But here's the twist. Sweden: four hundred eighty days of paid leave, shared by both parents, and you can save some days until your child turns twelve! How long should parental leave be?"+C,
'incometax_dyk':"What if you paid zero income tax? Let's compare the top rates. America: thirty seven percent federal, plus state tax in most states. Australia: forty five percent, on income over one hundred ninety thousand dollars. The UK: forty five percent too, on income over about one hundred twenty five thousand pounds. Japan: forty five percent, plus a ten percent local tax. Denmark: around fifty six percent, but healthcare and college are free! But here's the twist. The UAE: zero percent personal income tax, so your whole salary goes home with you! Would you move for zero tax?"+C,
'pizza_dyk':"Is Hawaiian pizza actually from Hawaii? Let's look at pizza around the world. America: pepperoni is the top topping, on about a third of all pizzas ordered. Australia: egg on pizza is a thing, and so is prawns! Japan: mayo, corn and even squid. Sweden: banana and curry pizza, and people actually love it! Italy: the Margherita, said to be named after a queen, with the colors of the Italian flag. But here's the twist. Canada: Hawaiian pizza was invented in Ontario in nineteen sixty two, by a Greek immigrant named Sam Panopoulos! Pineapple on pizza, yes or no?"+C,
'languages_dyk':"What if your country had more than twenty languages? Let's count. America: English became the official language in twenty twenty five, by executive order. Australia: no official language at all, even though almost everyone speaks English! Canada: two, English and French, and every product label uses both. Switzerland: four, German, French, Italian and Romansh. South Africa: twelve, including sign language, added in twenty twenty three! But here's the twist. India: twenty two languages listed in its constitution, hundreds more spoken every day, and banknotes show writing in fifteen of them! How many languages do you speak?"+C,
'sports_dyk':"What if every country loved the same sport? Let's see. America: American football, and the Super Bowl is the biggest TV event every year! Australia: Aussie rules football, the AFL, played on giant oval fields. Canada: ice hockey, and kids start skating almost as soon as they walk. India: cricket, with over a billion fans and players treated like movie stars. Brazil: soccer, with a record five World Cup wins! But here's the twist. New Zealand: rugby, the All Blacks do a haka before every match, and they've won over three quarters of their games! What's your favorite sport?"+C,
'tea_dyk':"What if you drank tea all day long? Let's compare tea per person. America: about a quarter kilo a year, and most of it is iced tea! Australia: about three quarters of a kilo. Japan: about one kilo, mostly green tea, and it even has a whole tea ceremony. The UK: almost two kilos, about one hundred million cups every single day! Ireland: about two point two kilos, even more than the UK! But here's the twist. Turkey: over three kilos per person, the biggest tea drinkers on Earth, served in little tulip shaped glasses! Tea or coffee?"+C,
'bikes_dyk':"What if there were more bikes than people? Let's ride. America: less than one percent of workers bike to work, most people drive. Australia: about one percent too, and wearing a helmet is the law. Germany: around one in ten trips by bike, with bike lanes in most cities. Japan: lots of mamachari, mom bikes with seats for kids and baskets for groceries! Denmark: in Copenhagen, almost half of commuters ride a bike, even in the snow! But here's the twist. The Netherlands: about twenty three million bikes, more bikes than people, and Utrecht has a parking garage for over twelve thousand bikes! Bike, car or bus?"+C,
'anthem_dyk':"What if your national anthem had a hundred and fifty eight verses? Let's listen. America: the Star Spangled Banner has four verses, and usually only the first is sung. Australia: changed one line in twenty twenty one, from young and free, to one and free. The UK: God Save the King, changed from Queen in twenty twenty two. Japan: Kimigayo, one of the shortest anthems in the world, with just thirty two syllables. Spain: the Marcha Real has no official words, so players just hum! But here's the twist. Greece: the Hymn to Liberty has one hundred fifty eight verses, the longest anthem on Earth, but only two are usually sung! Do you know your anthem by heart?"+C,
'independence_dyk':"What if your country had no independence day? Let's check. America: July fourth, seventeen seventy six, with fireworks and barbecues. Mexico: September sixteenth, when people shout Viva Mexico the night before. Canada: Canada Day, July first, for confederation in eighteen sixty seven. India: August fifteenth, nineteen forty seven, with kites flying everywhere. Australia: no independence day, Australia Day is January twenty sixth, when the First Fleet arrived. But here's the twist. The UK: no independence day either, and more than sixty countries celebrate independence from it! Does your country have one?"+C,
'weekend_dyk':"What if your weekend was Friday and Saturday? Let's compare. America: Saturday and Sunday, a two day weekend that spread after Henry Ford gave factory workers two days off in nineteen twenty six. Australia: Saturday and Sunday too, often spent at the beach or watching sport. Saudi Arabia: Friday and Saturday, since twenty thirteen. Egypt: Friday and Saturday too, with Friday prayers at the heart of the week. Kuwait: also Friday and Saturday. But here's the twist. The UAE: it switched to a Saturday and Sunday weekend in twenty twenty two, with a half day on Friday, to match the rest of the world's markets! Which days should be the weekend?"+C,
}
